<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">20 January 1961 · Washington, D.C.</p><h1 class="sp-title">Kennedy: Ask Not What Your Country Can Do for You</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>John F. Kennedy, at forty-three the youngest man ever elected to the presidency, gave his inaugural address at the height of the Cold War, calling a new generation to public service.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>And so, my fellow Americans: ask not what your country can do for you — ask what you can do for your country.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Inaugural Address, 20 January 1961.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/John_F._Kennedy%27s_Inaugural_Address" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>Kennedy's presidency saw the Bay of Pigs invasion, the Cuban Missile Crisis and the start of the race to the Moon. He was assassinated in Dallas in November 1963.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-pigs-invasion">Bay of Pigs invasion</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis">Cuban Missile Crisis</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/space-race">Space Race</a></div></div></section>
